  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Oil Wealth and Development in Uganda and Beyond is showing clear early uptake as a policy-relevant scholarly book. It has been downloaded 5,201 times, has 4 OpenAlex citations, and is aligned with SDG 8 on decent work and economic growth, indicating that it is being used both in research and in conversations about resource governance and development.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2019, Oil Wealth and Development in Uganda and Beyond examines how Uganda prepared to manage newly discovered oil resources, with wider relevance for other African countries facing similar extractive-sector challenges. The record shows a book positioned at the intersection of economics, business, and public policy, with particular relevance to sustainability and development debates. It is openly available in hybrid form, which likely supports broader access beyond a narrow specialist audience.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is usage: 5,201 downloads suggest sustained reader interest and practical demand for the book\u2019s analysis. That attention is complemented by a small but substantive citation record. The book has been cited in work appearing across related areas such as climate policy, African studies, political science, and political economy, showing that its framing of oil, governance, and development has been taken up in multiple adjacent fields.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing literature points to a particularly clear thematic footprint. Subsequent works engage the book through questions of mining and resource management, natural resources and economic development, and international development and aid, with some citations also connecting to climate justice, political support, and governance reform. This pattern suggests that the book is serving as a reference point for researchers examining extractive resources not only as an economic issue, but also as a political and developmental one.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader dissemination record also supports its scholarly reach. The book is held in library systems and associated with subject areas including environmental policy, energy industries, development economics, and energy and natural resources law, which is consistent with use in teaching and research collections. While the citation base is still modest, the combination of downloads, cross-disciplinary citations, open availability, and policy relevance indicates an active and credible early footprint for the book.</p>",
